\begin_layout Section*

\bar under
Section 3.2.3, Problem 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Prove that: (a) Strict monotonicity implies monotonicity; (b) Monotonicity
 implies local non-satiation; (c) The converse statements are not true.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(a) Strict monotonicity implies monotonicity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let the binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 be strictly monotone
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\gg y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $x\gg y\implies x\gneq y\implies x\succ y$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is strictly monotone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\gg y$
\end_inset

, we have that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succ y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is monotone
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Q.E.D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(b) Monotonicity implies local non-satiation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let the binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 be monotone
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Take 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and take 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, define 
\begin_inset Formula $y\equiv\left(y_{i}=x_{i}+\frac{\epsilon}{2}\right),\ i=i,\ldots,L$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in B_{\epsilon}^{X}(x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\gg x$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\implies y\succ x$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is monotone
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \epsilon\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ y\in B_{\epsilon}^{X}(x):y\succ x$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is locally non-satiated
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Q.E.D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(c) The converse statements are not true
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*

\bar under
Section 3.2.3, Problem 4
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Prove that continuity and continuity* are not necessarily equivlatent for
 binary relations that are complete but not, transitive, that is construct
 a complete and continuous* binary relation that is not continuous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Proof:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\left[0,1\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We will now define a binary relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 that is complete, intransitive, continuous*, but not continuous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq\equiv\left\{ (x,y)\in X^{2}:x_{1}\geq x_{2}\right\} \cup\left\{ (x,y)\in X^{2}:y=1-x,\ x>\frac{1}{4}\right\} $
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \left\{ x,y\right\} \subseteq X$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succeq y\iff[x\geq y]\ \vee\ \left[(y=1-x)\ \wedge\ \left(x>\frac{1}{4}\right)\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in[0,1],\ L\left(\succeq,x\right)\equiv\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
[0,x] & \mbox{if}\ x\leq\frac{1}{4}\\
{}[0,x]\cup\{1-x\} & \mbox{if}\ x>\frac{1}{4}
\end{array}\right\} $
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ y\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succeq,y)\equiv\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
[1,y] & \mbox{if}\ y\geq\frac{3}{4}\\
{}[1,y]\cup\{1-y\} & \mbox{if}\ y<\frac{3}{4}
\end{array}\right\} $
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in[0,1],\ U(\succeq,x)\ \mbox{and}\ L(\succeq,x)$
\end_inset

 are closed sets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is continuous*
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x^{t},y^{t}\right)=\left(\frac{1}{4},\frac{3}{4}\right)+\left(\frac{1}{2t},-\frac{1}{2t}\right),\ \forall\ t\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x^{t},y^{t}\right)\rightarrow\left(\frac{1}{4},\frac{3}{4}\right)=(x,y)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ t\in\mathbb{N},\ x^{t}\succeq y^{t}$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}>\frac{1}{4}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{t}=1-x^{t}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\nsucceq y$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $x=\frac{1}{4}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\frac{1}{4},\frac{3}{4}\right)\notin\succsim$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is not continuous
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Q.E.D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*

\bar under
Section 3.2.3, Problem 6
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Construct a quasilinear preference that is not continuous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and define 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq_{lex}$
\end_inset

 as follows: for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\equiv(x_{1},x_{2})\in\mathbb{R}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $y\equiv(y_{1},y_{2})\in\mathbb{R}^{2},\ x\succeq_{lex}y\iff\left[\left(x_{1}>y_{1}\right)\ \vee\ \left(x_{1}=y_{1}\ \wedge\ x_{2}\geq y_{2}\right)\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note, with this preference relation, indifference sets are just singletons.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq_{lex}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 is quasilinear with respect to commodity 1:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ (x_{1},x_{2})\in\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{+},\ (x_{1},x_{2})\sim(x_{1},x_{2})$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R},\ (x_{1}+\alpha,x_{2})\sim(x_{1}+\alpha,x_{2})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ (x_{1},x_{2})\in\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}_{+},\ \forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{++},(x_{1}+\alpha,x_{2})\succ(x_{1},x_{2})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq_{lex}$
\end_inset

 is not a continuous preference.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*

\bar under
Section 3.2.3, Problem 8
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider the preferences on 
\begin_inset Formula $[1,2]\subseteq\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 that are represented by the functions in Figure 3.5
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(a) Is the preference in Figure 3.5 (a) continuous? Formally support your
 answer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since we have a preference, then continuity* is equivalent to continuity
 by Theorem 7.
 So, to see if the preference is continuous, we will check to see if the
 preference is continuous*, i.e., if 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in[1,2],\ U(\succsim,x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\succsim,x)$
\end_inset

 are closed in 
\begin_inset Formula $[1,2]$
\end_inset

.
 After analyzing the graph, the two discontinuities occur at 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{5}{4}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{7}{4}$
\end_inset

 .
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $L\left(\succsim,\frac{5}{4}\right)\equiv\left\{ x\in[1,2]:\frac{5}{4}\succsim x\right\} =\left[1,\frac{5}{4}\right]\cup\left[\frac{7}{4},2\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $U\left(\succsim,\frac{5}{4}\right)\equiv\left\{ x\in[1,2]:x\succsim\frac{5}{4}\right\} =\left[\frac{5}{4},\frac{7}{4}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $L\left(\succsim,\frac{7}{4}\right)\equiv\left\{ x\in[1,2]:\frac{7}{4}\succsim x\right\} =\left[1,\frac{5}{4}\right]\cup\left[\frac{7}{4},2\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $U\left(\succeq,\frac{7}{4}\right)\equiv\left\{ x\in[1,2]:x\succsim\frac{5}{4}\right\} =\left[\frac{5}{4},\frac{7}{4}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note, all the sets listed above are closed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in[1,2],\ U(\succsim,x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\succsim,x)$
\end_inset

 are closed in 
\begin_inset Formula $[1,2]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous*
\begin_inset Formula $\iff$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Also, note that we can take an increasing function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, and transform the utility function as follows
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By Proposition 2 in the class notes, 
\begin_inset Formula $f\circ u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
 Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $f\circ u$
\end_inset

 is a continuous function, so by Proposition 7 in the class notes, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\series bold
Q.E.D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\series bold
(b) Is the preference in Figure 3.5(b) continuous? Formally support your
 answer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since we have a preference, then continuity* is equivalent to continuity
 by Theorem 7.
 So, to see if the preference is continuous, we will check to see if the
