\begin_layout Section*

\bar under
Section 3.2.3, Problem 9
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose that the set of alternatives is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 A preference 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
single peaked
\series default
 if there is 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

such that:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ x,y\right\} \subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x<y\leq x^{*},\ y\ P\ x$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ x,y\right\} \subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\leq y<x,\ y\ P\ x$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(a) Show that if 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is continuous, then it can be represented by a function 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 such that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\leq x^{*},\ u(x)=x$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is continuous, it has a continuous utility representation 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 by Theorem 9 (Debreu's Representation Theorem).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \left\{ x,y\right\} \subseteq\left[0,x^{*}\right],\ v(x)\geq v(y)\iff x\succeq y\iff x\geq y$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is single peaked at 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $v(x)$
\end_inset

 is monotonically equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[0,x^{*}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, there is a monotone function 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in\left[0,x^{*}\right],\ \varphi(v(x))=x$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)=\varphi(v(x))\ \forall\ x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 by Proposition 2 since 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi$
\end_inset

 is monotone
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in\left[0,x^{*}\right],\ u(x)=\varphi(v(x))=x$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Q.E.D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(b) Show that if 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is not continuous, then it may not be represented by a function 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 such that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\leq x^{*},\ u(x)=x$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq^{d}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 be represented by the utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)=\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
x & \mbox{if}\ x<1\\
x+1 & \mbox{if}\ 1\leq x\leq2\\
3-x & \mbox{if}\ 2\leq x<3\\
2-x & \mbox{if}\ 3\leq x
\end{array}\right.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq^{d}$
\end_inset

 is discontinuous since 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succeq^{d},1)$
\end_inset

 is not closed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose for contradiction 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq^{d}$
\end_inset

 has a utility representation 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in\left[0,x^{*}\right],\ u(x)=x$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq^{d}$
\end_inset

 , it must be monotonically equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

on 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[0,x^{*}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, there is a monotone function 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in\left[0,x^{*}\right],\ \varphi(f(x))=u(x)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ z\in[0,1),\ \varphi(z)=z$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ z\in[2,3],\ \varphi(z)=z-1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\leq2,\ u(x)=\varphi(f(x))=x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\geq2,\ u(x)=\varphi(f(x))=4-x$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)$
\end_inset

 is continuous on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But, this contradicts the discontinuity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq^{d}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq^{d}$
\end_inset

 must not have any utility representation 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in\left[0,x^{*}\right],\ u(x)=x$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Q.E.D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(c) Describe a situation in which it is reasonable to assume that the set
 of alternatives is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 and preferences are single peaked.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When comparing candidates for political office, it is reasonable to assume
 the set of alternatives is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 and preferences are single peaked.
 Many voters prefer a candidate that is more moderate to candidates that
 reside on either extremes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Eating is another situation in which it is reasonable to assume the set
 of alternatives is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 and preferences are single peaked.
 People prefer more food to less food up until the point where they have
 completely satiated themselves.
 After this point, more food is no longer ideal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*

\bar under
Section 3.3.3, Problem 11
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 be a monotone and continuous preference on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mbox{\mathbb{R}}_{++}^{L}.$
\end_inset

 Prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is represented by the function 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}$
\end_inset

 that associates with each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 the value
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}(x)\equiv\begin{array}{c}
\mbox{min}\\
y\in U(\succeq,x)
\end{array}p\cdot y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(a) Write the statement 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as an if and only if statement.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \left\{ x,y\right\} \subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succeq y\iff m_{p}(x)\geq m_{p}(y)$
\end_inset


\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(b) Provide an explicit proof of 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\Longrightarrow\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succeq y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
Want to show:
\series default
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}(x)\geq m_{p}(y)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in\ \arg{\displaystyle \min_{z\in U(\succsim,x)}}\ p\cdot z$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}\in\ \arg{\displaystyle \min_{t\in U(\succsim,y)}}\ p\cdot t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}(x)=p\cdot x^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}(y)=p\cdot y^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\succeq x\succeq y\implies x^{*}\succeq y$
\end_inset

 by transitivity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\implies x^{*}\in U(\succeq,y)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\implies U(\succsim,x)\subseteq U(\succsim,y)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\implies m_{p}(x)=p\cdot x^{*}\geq\begin{array}{c}
\mbox{min}\\
t\in U(\succeq,y)
\end{array}p\cdot t=m_{p}(y)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Q.E.D.
\end_layout

